[44/92] SuperGPQA/Science Among the following compounds, which one has the highest
The reactivity in hydrolysis under alkaline conditions depends on the ease of nucleophilic aromatic substitution (SNAr). This is favored by electron-withdrawing groups (especially nitro groups) that stabilize the intermediate Meisenheimer complex, and by leaving groups that are good (chloride). The more electron-withdrawing substituents (ortho and para to the leaving group), the higher the reactivity.

Among the options, **2,4-dinitrochlorobenzene** has two nitro groups ortho and para to chlorine, strongly activating the ring for SNAr. **2,6-dinitrochlorobenzene** also has two nitro groups ortho to chlorine, but the 2,4-isomer is slightly more reactive due to para nitro group. However, 2,4-dinitrochlorobenzene is known to be extremely reactive in alkaline hydrolysis.

Comparing all, **A (2,4-dinitrochlorobenzene)** has the highest reactivity.

