[61/92] GPQA Diamond/Chemistry The Michael reaction is a chemical process in organic chemistry
We need to determine the Michael addition products.

**First reaction:**  
The Michael donor is methyl 2-oxocyclohexane-1-carboxylate (a β-keto ester). Under NaOEt, it forms an enolate. The acceptor is 2,4-dimethyl-1-(vinylsulfinyl)benzene (a vinyl sulfoxide). The enolate adds to the β-carbon of the vinyl group, giving a new C–C bond at the α-position of the keto ester. That means the addition occurs at the carbon *adjacent* to the ester/ketone, which is the 1-position of the cyclohexane ring. So the product is methyl 1-(2-((2,4-dimethylphenyl)sulfinyl)ethyl)-2-oxocyclohexane-1-carboxylate.

**Second reaction:**  
Ethyl 2-ethylbutanoate is the donor (α-position to the carbonyl). NaH deprotonates it. The acceptor is methyl 2-cyclopentylidene-2-phenylacetate (an α,β-unsaturated ester). The enolate adds to the β-carbon of the unsaturated ester, forming a new bond at the α-carbon of the acceptor. The product is ethyl 2-ethyl-2-(1-(2-methoxy-2-oxo-1-phenylethyl)cyclopentyl)butanoate.

Comparing with choices, that matches option A.

**Final answer:**  
Answer: A
