Cyclooctatetraene reacts with maleic anhydride via a *trans* addition (it is not aromatic; it undergoes Diels–Alder as a diene). The initial adduct has a bicyclic structure with two fused rings. After methanolysis, the anhydride becomes a dimethyl ester.  

Then cyclopentadiene adds to the remaining double bond(s) in the eight-membered ring portion. The stereochemistry is determined by the *endo* rule and the existing chiral centers.  

Comparing the SMILES and names, the correct major isomer should have the ester groups on the same side relative to the new cage system, and the cyclopentadiene adds *endo* with respect to the existing bridge.  

Option A has the correct relative configuration for the major *endo* product.  

