[73/92] GPQA Diamond/Physics Consider an oscillating charge distribution, which is spheroid
We need to determine the angular dependence and wavelength scaling for radiation from an oscillating spheroidal charge distribution.  
For a radiating source that is not a simple dipole, the angular distribution can vary.  
If the spheroid oscillates along the z-axis, the radiation pattern is symmetric about the z-axis.  
For a dipole aligned along z, the radiated power per solid angle goes as \(\sin^2\theta\), so at \(\theta=30^\circ\), \(\sin^2 30^\circ = 1/4\) of the maximum (which occurs at \(\theta=90^\circ\)).  
The wavelength dependence for dipole radiation goes as \(\lambda^{-4}\) (from the Larmor formula and \(P \propto \omega^4 \propto \lambda^{-4}\)).  

Thus the fraction is \(1/4\) and the form is \(\lambda^{-4}\).  

Answer: A
