The function \(f(x) = \frac{(x-a)(x-b)(x-c)(x-k)}{x}\) with \(a,b,c\) fixed.  
We can write  
\[
f(x) = (x-a)(x-b)(x-c)(x-k)/x.
\]  
For large \(x\), \(f(x) \sim x^3\) → ∞. For \(x\to 0^+\), \(f(x) \sim \frac{abc k}{x}\) → ∞. So there is at least one minimum.  
The critical points satisfy \(x P'(x) = P(x)\). This is equivalent to  
\[
\sum \frac{1}{x - r_i} = \frac{1}{x}.
\]  
That is  
\[
\frac{1}{x-18} + \frac{1}{x-72} + \frac{1}{x-98} + \frac{1}{x-k} = \frac{1}{x}.
\]  
Multiply through:  
\[
\frac{1}{x-18} + \frac{1}{x-72} + \frac{1}{x-98} + \frac{1}{x-k} - \frac{1}{x} = 0.
